<article class="env-definition" id="0FQH">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FQH">Definition <span data-tag="0FQH">22.28.1</span>.</a> Bimodules. Let $R$ be a ring. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Let $A$ and $B$ be $R$-algebras. An <em>$(A, B)$-bimodule</em> is an $R$-module $M$ equippend with $R$-bilinear maps 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  A \times M \to M, (a, x) \mapsto ax \quad \text{and}\quad M \times B \to M, (x, b) \mapsto xb  \]
</div>
<p>
 such that the following hold 
</p>
<ol>
  <li><p>
$a'(ax) = (a'a)x$ and $(xb)b' = x(bb')$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$a(xb) = (ax)b$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$1 x = x = x 1$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li><li><p>
Let $A$ and $B$ be $\mathbf{Z}$-graded $R$-algebras. A <em>graded $(A, B)$-bimodule</em> is an $(A, B)$-bimodule $M$ which has a grading $M = \bigoplus M^ n$ such that $A^ n M^ m \subset M^{n + m}$ and $M^ n B^ m \subset M^{n + m}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $A$ and $B$ be differential graded $R$-algebras. A <em>differential graded $(A, B)$-bimodule</em> is a graded $(A, B)$-bimodule which comes equipped with a differential $\text{d} : M \to M$ homogeneous of degree $1$ such that $\text{d}(ax) = \text{d}(a)x + (-1)^{\deg (a)}a\text{d}(x)$ and $\text{d}(xb) = \text{d}(x)b + (-1)^{\deg (x)}x\text{d}(b)$ for homogeneous elements $a \in A$, $x \in M$, $b \in B$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>